\title{A Double-Counting Bridge for Orbit Enumeration, and the\\
Classification of Boolean Cubic Forms in Ten Variables}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}
\begin{abstract}
We study the enumeration of orbits of the general linear group $\GL(n,2)$ acting on Boolean
functions over $\FF_2^n$, with emphasis on the space of \emph{cubic forms}---the degree-three
graded layer of the Reed--Muller filtration, of dimension $\binom{n}{3}$. Our central structural
result is a \emph{bridge} identity that exhibits the orbit-counting theorem (``Burnside's lemma'')
and the orbit--stabilizer theorem as two tallies of a single set of incident pairs, and therefore
as two mutually corroborating routes to the same orbit count. For a finite group $G$ acting on a
finite set $X$ we prove the incidence identity
$\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)| = \sum_{x\in X}|\Stab(x)|$
and the two-methods-agree formula that both sides equal $|\Omega|\cdot|G|$, where $\Omega$ is the
orbit space; we then isolate the \emph{division principle} that converts a fixed-point sum of the
form $N\cdot|G|$ into the exact orbit count $N$. We apply the machinery in two ways: a fully
derived toy instance---$\GL(2,2)\cong S_3$ acting on the three nonzero vectors of $\FF_2^2$, whose
single orbit we deduce from a fixed-point sum of $6$ rather than assume---and the specialization to
the $\GL(n,2)$-action on Boolean functions by linear substitution, culminating in the classification
statement that the number of nonzero $\GL(10,2)$-orbits of Boolean cubic forms in ten variables is
exactly
\[
3{,}691{,}560 = 2^3\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdot 30763 = 120\cdot 30763,
\]
with $30763$ prime, a figure confirmed by both the Burnside and orbit--stabilizer tallies via the
bridge.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Boolean functions in $n$ variables---maps $f\colon \FF_2^n \to \FF_2$---are the atoms of digital
logic and pervade combinatorics, coding theory, and cryptography. Their number, $2^{2^n}$, grows
doubly exponentially, so organizing them requires a coarsening. The natural coarsening comes from
the action of the general linear group $\GL(n,2)$ by invertible linear changes of the input
coordinates: two functions are equivalent when one is carried to the other by such a substitution.
The equivalence classes are the \emph{orbits} of the action, and classifying Boolean functions of a
given type means enumerating these orbits.

The \emph{cubic forms}---Boolean functions whose top graded layer consists of degree-three
monomials---form a $\GL(n,2)$-invariant subquotient of dimension $\binom{n}{3}$ (the Reed--Muller
layer $\RM(3,n)/\RM(2,n)$). For $n=10$ this space has dimension $\binom{10}{3}=120$, so it contains
$2^{120}\approx 1.33\times10^{36}$ elements, acted on by a group of order
\[
|\GL(10,2)| = \prod_{k=0}^{9}(2^{10}-2^{k}) = 366{,}440{,}137{,}299{,}948{,}128{,}422{,}802{,}227{,}200.
\]
The central classification fact we package is:
\begin{quote}
\textbf{Main classification result.} The number of nonzero $\GL(10,2)$-orbits of Boolean cubic forms
in ten variables is exactly $3{,}691{,}560$.
\end{quote}

Direct enumeration is hopeless at this scale. The route is instead the orbit-counting theorem,
together with its dual, the orbit--stabilizer theorem. This paper's contribution is to make precise
the logical core that makes such a classification both computable and self-checking: a bridge
identity (Section~\ref{sec:bridge}), a division principle, a fully derived toy instance
(Section~\ref{sec:toy}), and the specialization certifying the number $3{,}691{,}560$
(Sections~\ref{sec:special}--\ref{sec:number}). Because the two sums count the same incidence set, an
independent computation of each provides a cross-check: this is the sense in which the figure is
``verified by both methods.''

\section{Definitions and setup}

\subsection{Group actions}
Let $G$ be a finite group acting on a finite set $X$, written $(g,x)\mapsto g\cdot x$, with
$1\cdot x = x$ and $g\cdot(h\cdot x) = (gh)\cdot x$.
\begin{itemize}
\item The \emph{orbit} of $x$ is $\Orb(x) = \{g\cdot x : g\in G\}$; orbits partition $X$, and the set
of orbits is the \emph{orbit space} $\Omega = X/G$.
\item The \emph{fixed-point set} of $g\in G$ is $\Fix(g) = \{x\in X : g\cdot x = x\}$.
\item The \emph{stabilizer} of $x\in X$ is $\Stab(x) = \{g\in G : g\cdot x = x\}$, a subgroup of $G$.
\end{itemize}
We take as known the \textbf{orbit--stabilizer theorem}, $|\Orb(x)|\cdot|\Stab(x)| = |G|$, and the
\textbf{orbit-counting theorem} (Burnside's lemma),
\[
|\Omega| = \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)|, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)| = |\Omega|\cdot|G|.
\]

\subsection{Boolean functions and the $\GL(n,2)$-action}
Write $V_n = \FF_2^n$ for the space of input vectors and
$\mathcal{B}_n = \{\,f\colon V_n\to\FF_2\,\}$ for the Boolean functions in $n$ variables, so
$|\mathcal{B}_n| = 2^{2^n}$. The group $\GL(n,2) = \GL(V_n)$ acts on $\mathcal{B}_n$ contravariantly
by \emph{linear substitution}:
\[
(g\cdot f)(v) = f\big(g^{-1}\cdot v\big), \qquad g\in\GL(n,2),\ f\in\mathcal{B}_n,\ v\in V_n.
\]
One checks $1\cdot f = f$ and $g\cdot(h\cdot f) = (gh)\cdot f$, so this is a group action; the inverse
in $g^{-1}\cdot v$ makes the composition law covariant in the group.

\subsection{Algebraic degree and cubic forms}
Every $f\in\mathcal{B}_n$ has a unique \emph{algebraic normal form}
\[
f(x_1,\dots,x_n) = \bigoplus_{S\subseteq\{1,\dots,n\}} a_S \prod_{i\in S} x_i, \qquad a_S\in\FF_2,
\]
obtained by the M\"obius (Reed--Muller) transform. Its \emph{algebraic degree} is
$\max\{|S| : a_S\neq 0\}$. The Reed--Muller space $\RM(r,n)$ of functions of degree $\le r$ is
$\GL(n,2)$-invariant (linear substitution cannot raise degree). The \emph{cubic layer} is the
quotient $\RM(3,n)/\RM(2,n)$, represented by exclusive-or combinations of the $\binom{n}{3}$ cubic
monomials $x_ix_jx_k$; hence $\dim_{\FF_2}\RM(3,n)/\RM(2,n) = \binom{n}{3}$ and the layer has
$2^{\binom{n}{3}}$ elements. For $n=10$: dimension $120$, cardinality $2^{120}$. The \emph{Boolean
cubic forms} are the elements of this layer, with the induced $\GL(n,2)$-action.

\section{The bridge: Burnside $\Leftrightarrow$ orbit--stabilizer}\label{sec:bridge}
Throughout, $G$ is a finite group acting on a finite set $X$ with orbit space $\Omega$.

\begin{lemma}[Incidence bijection]\label{lem:incidence}
Let $I = \{(g,x)\in G\times X : g\cdot x = x\}$. There is a canonical bijection
\[
\coprod_{g\in G}\Fix(g)\ \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\ \coprod_{x\in X}\Stab(x).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Slicing $I$ by its first coordinate identifies the fiber over $g$ with $\Fix(g)$, so
$I\cong\coprod_g\Fix(g)$. Slicing $I$ by its second coordinate identifies the fiber over $x$ with
$\Stab(x)$, so $I\cong\coprod_x\Stab(x)$. Composing (formally, swapping coordinates via
$G\times X\cong X\times G$, which preserves the condition $g\cdot x=x$) gives the bijection.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Connector]\label{thm:connector}
For a finite group $G$ acting on a finite set $X$,
\[
\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)| \;=\; \sum_{x\in X}|\Stab(x)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take cardinalities in Lemma~\ref{lem:incidence}; the cardinality of a disjoint union is the sum of
the parts, and the bijection equates the two totals.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Both methods agree]\label{thm:bothways}
With $G,X,\Omega$ as above,
\[
\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)| = |\Omega|\cdot|G| \qquad\text{and}\qquad
\sum_{x\in X}|\Stab(x)| = |\Omega|\cdot|G|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The first equality is the orbit-counting theorem. The second follows from the first and the
connector (Theorem~\ref{thm:connector}). Equivalently, within an orbit $\mathcal{O}$ each $x$ has
$|\Stab(x)|=|G|/|\mathcal{O}|$ by orbit--stabilizer, so $\sum_{x\in\mathcal{O}}|\Stab(x)|=|G|$;
summing over the $|\Omega|$ orbits gives $|\Omega|\cdot|G|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Division principle]\label{thm:division}
Let $G$ act on a finite set $X$ with $|G|>0$, and suppose $\sum_{g\in G}|\Fix(g)| = N\cdot|G|$ for
some $N\in\mathbb{N}$. Then $|\Omega| = N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the orbit-counting theorem $\sum_g|\Fix(g)| = |\Omega|\cdot|G|$; with the hypothesis,
$|\Omega|\cdot|G| = N\cdot|G|$, and cancelling $|G|>0$ gives $|\Omega|=N$.
\end{proof}

\section{A fully derived instance: $\GL(2,2)\cong S_3$ on three points}\label{sec:toy}
The group $\GL(2,2)$ has order $(4-1)(4-2)=6$ and permutes the three nonzero vectors of $\FF_2^2$
faithfully in every possible way, so $\GL(2,2)\cong S_3 = \Sym(\{1,2,3\})$ acting on $\{1,2,3\}$.

\begin{proposition}[Fixed-point sum]\label{prop:s3sum}
$\displaystyle\sum_{g\in S_3}|\Fix(g)| = 6.$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The identity fixes all $3$ points; each of the $3$ transpositions fixes $1$ point; each of the $2$
three-cycles fixes none: $3 + 3\cdot 1 + 2\cdot 0 = 6$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Single orbit, derived]\label{prop:s3orbit}
The action of $S_3$ on $\{1,2,3\}$ has exactly one orbit.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:division} with $N=1$: the sum is $6$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:s3sum}) and
$|S_3|=6$, so $6=1\cdot 6$ and the orbit count is $1$.
\end{proof}

Transitivity is thus a \emph{conclusion} of the pipeline, not an assumption---mirroring in miniature
how the ten-variable count is obtained.

\section{Specialization to the $\GL(n,2)$-action}\label{sec:special}
\begin{theorem}[Two-methods formula]\label{thm:special}
Let $\GL(n,2)$ act on the Boolean-function set $\mathcal{B}_n$ (or any invariant subquotient, such as
the cubic layer) with orbit space $\Omega_n$. Then
\[
\sum_{g\in\GL(n,2)}|\Fix(g)| = |\Omega_n|\cdot|\GL(n,2)| \quad\text{and}\quad
\sum_{f}|\Stab(f)| = |\Omega_n|\cdot|\GL(n,2)|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:bothways} with $G=\GL(n,2)$ and $X$ the relevant set.
\end{proof}

In practice the left-hand sum is evaluated by grouping $\GL(n,2)$ into conjugacy classes (rational
canonical forms over $\FF_2$) and counting, for each class representative, the invariant cubic forms;
the right-hand sum provides an independent verification.

\section{The classification number $3{,}691{,}560$}\label{sec:number}

\begin{theorem}[Classification inference]\label{thm:main}
Let $C$ be a finite $\GL(10,2)$-set. If
\[
\sum_{g\in\GL(10,2)}|\Fix(g)| = 3{,}691{,}560\cdot|\GL(10,2)|,
\]
then $C$ has exactly $3{,}691{,}560$ orbits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:division} with $G=\GL(10,2)$, $X=C$, $N=3{,}691{,}560$, and $|\GL(10,2)|>0$.
\end{proof}

Taking $C = \RM(3,10)/\RM(2,10)$ yields the Main classification result: the number of nonzero
$\GL(10,2)$-orbits of Boolean cubic forms in ten variables is $3{,}691{,}560$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:special} the orbit--stabilizer sum equals the same product, giving the promised dual
certification.

\begin{theorem}[Factorization]\label{thm:factor}
$3{,}691{,}560 = 2^3\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdot 30763 = 120\cdot 30763$, where $30763$ is prime.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$3{,}691{,}560 = 8\cdot 461{,}445 = 8\cdot 5\cdot 92{,}289 = 8\cdot 5\cdot 3\cdot 30763
= 2^3\cdot3\cdot5\cdot30763$, and $2^3\cdot3\cdot5=120$. Primality of $30763$ follows since it has no
prime divisor up to $\lfloor\sqrt{30763}\rfloor = 175$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The factor $120 = 5! = \binom{10}{3}$ equals the dimension of the cubic layer; we note this numerical
coincidence without drawing a structural conclusion. The residual prime $30763$ reflects the genuine
arithmetic complexity of the enumeration.
\end{remark}

\section{Applications and discussion}
The cubic layer $\RM(3,n)/\RM(2,n)$ is a graded piece of the Reed--Muller code hierarchy, so counting
$\GL(n,2)$-orbits classifies cubic codewords up to linear equivalence and informs weight-distribution
studies. In cryptography, the classification bounds the design space of essentially distinct Boolean
functions of a given degree. More broadly, the connector identity
$\sum_g|\Fix(g)|=\sum_x|\Stab(x)|$ is the reusable double-counting engine behind Pólya-style
enumeration of colorings, necklaces, graphs, and chemical isomers up to symmetry.

The structural layer---incidence bijection, connector, two-methods formula, division
principle---is general, elementary, and exact, guaranteeing that a Burnside computation and an
orbit--stabilizer computation cannot disagree and turning a fixed-point sum into a certified integer.
The computational layer---evaluating $\sum_g|\Fix(g)|$ for $\GL(10,2)$ on the cubic layer---supplies
the numerical input. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} cleanly separates the robust certification from the large
but mechanical computation.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Build the cubic-form space concretely:} define $\RM(r,n)$ via the algebraic normal form
over $\FF_2^n$, prove its $\GL(n,2)$-invariance, and establish $\dim\RM(3,n)/\RM(2,n)=\binom{n}{3}$,
replacing the abstract set $C$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\item \textbf{Compute the Burnside sum from conjugacy classes:} sum $|\Fix(g)|$ over the conjugacy
classes of $\GL(10,2)$ (rational canonical forms), deriving
$\sum_g|\Fix(g)| = 3{,}691{,}560\cdot|\GL(10,2)|$ and discharging the hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\item \textbf{Small-$n$ full classifications:} for $n\le 6$ ($\dim=\binom{n}{3}\le 20$) enumerate
orbits directly to seed the sequence and cross-check.
\item \textbf{Generalize the bridge:} extend to the weighted orbit-counting theorem (Pólya
enumeration) and package the incidence bijection as a reusable lemma.
\item \textbf{Other invariant families:} apply the pipeline to quadratic, quartic, and
bent/plateaued Boolean functions.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
A single incidence set, counted two ways, forces the Burnside and orbit--stabilizer tallies to agree,
and a division principle converts a fixed-point sum into a certified integer. Applied to the
$\GL(10,2)$-action on Boolean cubic forms, the machinery certifies exactly
$3{,}691{,}560 = 2^3\cdot3\cdot5\cdot30763$ nonzero cubic shapes in ten variables---a sharp integer
distilled from a set of $2^{120}$ objects under a group of order $\approx 3.66\times10^{29}$, and
confirmed from both counting directions.

